<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<ValidationRule xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        THIS NOTE LIVES IN A COMMENT, NOT IN <description>, AND IT MUST STAY THAT WAY.
        ValidationRule.Description is capped at 255 characters and the rationale below is far
        longer; a comment is repo-only and is never sent to the Metadata API. NOTE ALSO: the
        comment must sit INSIDE the root element. A comment placed ABOVE <ValidationRule>
        breaks `sf` deploy at conversion with a misleading "unable to find matching parent xml
        file" error.

        =====================================================================================
        ADDED 2026-08-20 (Tranche 2, Workstream A1). THE FIRST VALIDATION RULE ON THIS OBJECT.
        =====================================================================================
        A broker response with no broker on it is not a response. BOV_Submission__c is the
        record of WHICH broker said WHAT, and the whole comparison matrix, the appointment
        flow (BovSubmissionService.replaceSelectedBroker) and the stamped denormalised text
        (BovSubmissionBrokerStampService) are all keyed on Broker__c. A row with a blank
        Broker__c carries a firm string and a contact name that nothing derived and nothing
        can re-derive.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 WHY THIS IS A VALIDATION RULE AND NOT `required=true` ON THE FIELD. TWO INDEPENDENT
        REASONS, EITHER OF WHICH IS SUFFICIENT.
        =====================================================================================
        1. THE DELETE CONSTRAINT. Salesforce does not permit deleteConstraint SetNull on a
           REQUIRED lookup; a required lookup must be Restrict. Broker__c is SetNull today and
           its own field header carries an explicit, reasoned DO NOT: switching to Restrict
           would block legitimate Contact deletion org wide and would diverge from all four
           sibling broker lookups. `required=true` here is therefore not an attribute flip,
           it is the schema change that header refuses.
        2. 🔴 IT WOULD BREAK BOTH DISPOSITION PERMISSION SETS. A universally required field
           cannot carry field level security, and the Metadata API rejects a fieldPermissions
           entry naming one. Every field this tranche makes "required" would have to be
           DELETED from DPEG_Disposition_Edit and DPEG_Disposition_View to keep them
           deployable. Measured 2026-08-20: BOV_Submission__c.BOV_Amount__c and
           Disposition_Offer__c.Offer_Amount__c are both granted in both sets, so the
           `required=true` route named in the tranche 2 requirements doc (§2 A1) for the two
           CURRENCY fields would have taken two grants with it. All four A1 requirements are
           validation rules for this reason; see the sibling rule files.

        A rule also stays scopeable later (exempt a status, exempt a record type) with a
        one file edit and no schema change on live data.

        =====================================================================================
        NO ISCHANGED / ISNEW GUARD, MATCHING Broker_Lookup_Is_Off_Market_Only.
        =====================================================================================
        This is an INVARIANT, not a transition: a BOV submission never legitimately holds a
        blank broker, at any point in its life. A transition guard would leave every
        pre existing blank row permanently invisible to the rule.
        🔴 AN UNGUARDED RULE CANNOT TRAP A RECORD HERE, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES IT SAFE. If a row
        is in the forbidden state every subsequent save fails, including SYSTEM_MODE machine
        writes (SYSTEM_MODE does NOT bypass validation rules). But the remedy, saving with a
        broker picked, is itself a PERMITTED save and the error message says so.
        ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-08-20: this line said Broker__c's lookupFilter tested
        Contact.Is_Broker__c. The broker definition moved to the Contact.Broker record type in
        the same tranche; Broker__c now carries an ACTIVE, non optional lookupFilter on
        Contact.RecordType.DeveloperName = 'Broker', so the remedy requires a Contact on that
        record type. That is the same population scripts/seed-broker-contacts.apex maintains.

        =====================================================================================
        🔴 MEASURED IMPACT AT CREATION: ONE EXISTING ROW IS REFUSED. NOT ZERO.
        =====================================================================================
        Queried on usman-dpeg, 2026-08-20:
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM BOV_Submission__c                          -> 1
          SELECT COUNT(Id) FROM BOV_Submission__c WHERE Broker__c = null   -> 1
        That single row is BOV-0014 (Submission_Status__c = 'Backup', Broker__c,
        Broker_Firm__c, Contact_Name__c and BOV_Amount__c all null). It is the live evidence
        that motivated this rule, and it violates this rule and
        BOV_Amount_Required_On_Submission both. From the moment this deploys, BOV-0014 cannot
        be saved until a broker Contact is chosen on it. It is a demo artefact carrying no
        broker and no amount, so DELETING it is the recommended remedy; repairing it needs a
        broker Contact that passes the lookupFilter above.
        ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-08-20: this line said "...that passes the Is_Broker__c filter." The
        broker definition moved to the Contact.Broker record type; the remedy is a Contact on
        that record type, not a checked Is_Broker__c box.
        🔴 THAT MEASUREMENT AGES. Re-run the count at deploy time rather than citing this
        line. A larger non zero result is an escalation, not a deploy.

        =====================================================================================
        ⚠ TEST AND SEED BLAST RADIUS, OWNED BY THE DEVELOPER AGENT, NOT BY THIS FILE.
        =====================================================================================
        TestDataFactory.createBovSubmissions sets Broker_Firm__c and Contact_Name__c and
        leaves Broker__c NULL, so every fixture it produces is refused the moment this
        deploys.

        🔴 CORRECTED 2026-08-20 (code-review finding C2). THE SEED SENTENCE NAMED THE WRONG
        FILES AND DEFERRED WORK THAT IS NOW DONE. Quoted and retracted in place rather than
        edited, because a reader who took the old list at face value needs to see that BOTH
        halves of it were wrong, not just that a number changed:

            RETRACTED: "scripts/seed-disposition-bulk.apex and
            scripts/seed-disposition-offers.apex break the same way. Those edits are
            requirements doc §3 D1 and are NOT part of this change. 🔴 DO NOT DEPLOY THIS
            RULE AHEAD OF THEM: RunLocalTests goes red across the whole BOV surface."

        WHAT WAS WRONG WITH IT:
          - scripts/seed-disposition-offers.apex CREATES NO BOV SUBMISSIONS AT ALL. Measured:
            zero occurrences of BOV_Submission__c in that file. It seeds Disposition_Offer__c
            and every row sets Buyer_Name__c and Offer_Amount__c, so it was already clean and
            was never in this rule's blast radius.
          - IT OMITTED TWO FILES THAT ARE. scripts/seed-disposition.apex (4 rows) and
            scripts/seed-disp0002.apex (4 rows) both built BOV submissions with a NULL
            Broker__c exactly as the bulk seed did.
          - THE Broker__c GREP THAT PRODUCED THE LIST MATCHED THE WRONG FIELD. The hits at
            seed-disposition-bulk.apex lines 68/75/80 are Disposition__c.Selected_Broker__c -
            a different field on a different object - not this lookup.

        THE SWEEP IS DONE, NOT DEFERRED. As of 2026-08-20 all THREE files that create BOV
        submissions resolve a broker Contact and set Broker__c:
            scripts/seed-disposition-bulk.apex   14 rows
            scripts/seed-disposition.apex         4 rows
            scripts/seed-disp0002.apex            4 rows
        Each queries the Contact population up front, BEFORE its first DML, and ABORTS naming
        scripts/seed-broker-contacts.apex if that population is empty - so a broker-less org
        fails loudly instead of half-seeding. Each row resolves by firm against that roster,
        because the lookupFilter above is active and non-optional. The scripts no longer set
        Broker_Firm__c or Contact_Name__c: BovSubmissionBrokerStampService overwrites both from
        the chosen Contact in the same before-save, so any literal there would be dead on
        arrival.
        ⚠ CORRECTED 2026-08-20: this line said "Each queries the Is_Broker__c = true Contact
        population..." All three scripts now query `WHERE RecordType.DeveloperName = 'Broker'`
        (verified directly against scripts/seed-disposition-bulk.apex:104,
        scripts/seed-disposition.apex:47 and scripts/seed-disp0002.apex:47), consistent with the
        broker definition's move to the Contact.Broker record type.

        WHAT REMAINS OWNED ELSEWHERE: TestDataFactory.createBovSubmissions and the BOV test
        classes. That is the unit-testing agent's work, not this file's and not the seed
        sweep's. 🔴 DO NOT DEPLOY THIS RULE AHEAD OF IT: RunLocalTests goes red across the
        whole BOV surface.
    -->
    <fullName>Broker_Required_On_Submission</fullName>
    <active>true</active>
    <description>A BOV submission must name the broker who responded. Broker__c keys the comparison matrix, the appointment flow and the stamped firm/contact text, none of which can be re-derived from a blank lookup. A rule, not required=true: see the XML comment.</description>
    <errorConditionFormula>ISBLANK(Broker__c)</errorConditionFormula>
    <errorMessage>Choose the Broker Contact who submitted this BOV. A submission with no broker cannot be compared or appointed. Only Broker Contacts can be selected here; if the broker is missing, create the Contact on the Broker record type first.</errorMessage>
    <errorDisplayField>Broker__c</errorDisplayField>
</ValidationRule>
